* PIC18(L)F4XK22 Pin Definitions -- 40/44-pin family
* Source: Microchip DS40001412G "PIC18(L)F2X/4XK22 Data Sheet", Table 1-3
*         (PIC18(L)F4XK22 Pinout I/O Descriptions)
*
* Flagged for manual review (dual-function / non-obvious pins):
*   RE3/VPP/MCLR   -- default function is MCLR (active-low device Reset);
*                     RE3 GPIO requires the MCLR Configuration bit cleared.
*                     Classified I (input-only) per the table's Pin Type
*                     column -- datasheet Features list confirms this is
*                     the device's single input-only pin (35 I/O + 1 input).
*   RA6/CLKO/OSC2, RA7/CLKI/OSC1 -- primary oscillator pins; unavailable as
*                     GPIO when a crystal/resonator/external-clock
*                     oscillator mode is selected.
*   RC0/SOSCO, RC1/SOSCI -- Timer1 secondary oscillator pins; same caveat.
*   RB6/PGC, RB7/PGD -- ICSP programming clock/data; shared with GPIO.
*   CCP2/P2A (RB3 vs RC1) and T3CKI/CCP3/P3A/P2B (RB5/RC0/RE0/RD2) --
*                     these alternate functions are pin-remapped by
*                     Configuration bits (CCP2MX, CCP3MX, T3CMX, PB2MX)
*                     rather than simultaneously present on every listed
*                     pin; see datasheet Notes 1/2 for default vs.
*                     alternate assignment. Recorded as-is on each physical
*                     pin -- mux selection is a runtime/config concern
*                     outside this file's scope.
*
* No directional-arrow Pin Diagram is present in this datasheet (Figures
* 3-6 show only base pin names -- e.g. "RA0", "MCLR/VPP/RE3" -- not full
* alt-function lists), so the Step 5 arrow cross-check does not apply.
* Table 1-3 pin-type language (I/O/P columns) is the sole classification
* source, and alt-function text below is taken from Table 1-3's pin-name
* header rows rather than the Pin Diagram figures.
*
* VDD and VSS each occupy two physical package pins (PDIP 11/32 and
* 12/31 respectively, TQFP/QFN equivalents per the table); both are the
* same net, so a single V/G record covers both -- no VDD2/VSS2 created.

